ehaved afterwards they said at best he  was utterly unconcerned and at worst he  was quite pleased with himself  because he felt big now the defendant  appeared throughout this trial via video  link uh he can't be named for legal  reasons and he was able to appear  remotely because of his age when that  verdict was read out in court he put his  head in his hands and he stayed with his  head in his hands for quite a while the  prosecution said during the trial that  he had covered up his tracks by lying  about his whereabouts that night denying  that he was even there concealing  evidence including the coat that he was  wearing and trying to blame someone else  before finally admitting that he had  been there and admitting that he'd  stabbed ava  and we were shown hours of cctv and that  is something that the cps when i spoke  to them earlier talked about the kind of  difficulty in going through all this  cctv to present it to the court the cps  also spoke about  the tragic nature of this case the fact  that the defendants are so young and  here uh is uh claire chicony from the  cps this is what she told me earlier  i think the worrying thing for people  dealing with this case was the age of  the victim and the age of the defendant  but also that there were a number of  other children present during the  incident so it was a it was a massive  concern it's obviously had devastating  effects  for everybody involved  and that that's what the cps were really  keen to talk about the devastating  impact on this case obviously primarily  on ava's family but also on the children  who witnessed the events that night and  of course on the defendant himself he is  just a 14 year old boy and i know that  ava's family have been very keen to talk  about knife crime in the past but that  is the thing to take away from this the  fact that a 14 year old boy was in town  with a knife and a 12 year old girl was  stabbed to death  becky that's utterly tragic and becky  crusher thank you
 

